Package: bayescompR
Title: Pure R Implementation of the 'Stan' Modeling Language
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    c(person("Adam R", "Sophie", email = "adamrsophie@gmail.com",
             role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
      person("Stan Development Team", role = "cph",
             comment = "function signature table and warning texts derived from stanc and CmdStan; see inst/COPYRIGHTS"))
Description: An independent implementation, written entirely in R, of a large
    subset of the 'Stan' probabilistic programming language (Carpenter et al.
    2017 <doi:10.18637/jss.v076.i01>) and of many inference algorithms.
    Programs are parsed into a typed syntax tree, checked by a semantic
    analyzer, and compiled into R closures that run on a reverse mode
    automatic differentiation tape. Constrained parameters are mapped to an
    unconstrained space with the Jacobian adjustments used by 'Stan'.
    Inference includes the No-U-Turn sampler (Hoffman and Gelman 2014
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.1111.4246>; Betancourt 2017
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.1701.02434>) with windowed step size and metric
    adaptation, static Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, Riemannian manifold samplers,
    microcanonical Langevin Monte Carlo, the Metropolis adjusted Langevin
    algorithm, adaptive random walk Metropolis, slice and elliptical slice
    sampling, blocked Gibbs sampling, ensemble samplers, non-reversible
    parallel tempering, zig-zag and bouncy particle samplers, sequential
    Monte Carlo, particle MCMC for state space models, stochastic gradient
    MCMC, expectation propagation, nested sampling, reversible jump MCMC,
    integrated nested Laplace approximation, optimization, Laplace
    approximation, mean field, full rank and normalizing flow variational
    inference, Pathfinder (Zhang et al. 2022
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.2108.03782>), neural posterior estimation
    and diffusion based sampling. A typed, versioned intermediate
    representation lets separately installed native backends compute the log
    density and its gradient. Posterior summaries use rank normalized split
    R-hat and bulk and tail effective sample sizes (Vehtari et al. 2021
    <doi:10.1214/20-BA1221>). No C++ compiler or 'Stan' installation is
    required. This package is not affiliated with or endorsed by the 'Stan'
    Development Team or 'NumFOCUS'.
